    "type": "test",
    "category": "Biochemistry",
    "rawText": "Physiological basis\nThe Absolute Eosinophil Count (AEC) is a measure of the number of eosinophils, a type of white blood cell, in a given volume of blood. The Absolute Eosinophil Count (AEC) is calculated by multiplying the percentage of eosinophils in the total white blood cell count by the total white blood cell count.\n\nInterpretation\n\nElevated AEC\nLow AEC Allergic Reactions\nParasitic Infections\nAutoimmune Diseases\nEosinophilia\nAcute Infections\nAdrenal Insufficiency\n\nComments\nThe AEC is typically assessed as part of a complete blood count (CBC) with a differential. If your AEC is outside the normal range, it's important to consult with a healthcare provider to determine the underlying cause and appropriate treatment.",
    "htmlContent": "<p><strong>Physiological basis</strong><br>The Absolute Eosinophil Count (AEC) is a measure of the number of eosinophils, a type of white blood cell, in a given volume of blood. The Absolute Eosinophil Count (AEC) is calculated by multiplying the percentage of eosinophils in the total white blood cell count by the total white blood cell count.<br><br><strong>Interpretation</strong></p>\n\n<strong>Elevated AEC</strong>\n<br><strong>Low AEC</strong>\n\nAllergic Reactions <br>Parasitic Infections <br>Autoimmune Diseases <br>Eosinophilia\n<br>Acute Infections <br>Adrenal Insufficiency\n\n<p><strong>Comments</strong><br>The AEC is typically assessed as part of a complete blood count (CBC) with a differential. If your AEC is outside the normal range, it's important to consult with a healthcare provider to determine the underlying cause and appropriate treatment.</p>"
