    "rawText": "2006 International Consensus statement on Classification of definite APS\n\n\nCLINICAL CRITERIA\nLABORATORY CRITERIA Arterial/Venous thrombosis\nCardiolipin antibodies (aCL) Fetal Loss\nBeta 2 Glycoprotein 1 antibodies Premature birth\nLupus Anticoagulant (LA)\n\n\nNote:\nAPS is diagnosed if at least one laboratory criterion and one clinical criterion are met. For the laboratory criteria, they need to be observed on two or more separate occasions, with each test done at least 12 weeks apart.\n\nComments\nThis test checks for IgG antibodies to Beta-2 glycoprotein. Finding these antibodies can indicate a risk of blood clots in arteries and veins, as well as a higher chance of repeated miscarriages. The new International classification criteria for APS include Beta-2 glycoprotein for 2 key reasons namely:\n1. Beta-2 Glycoprotein antibodies maybe the only antibody present in 10% patients of Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS)\n2. Beta-2 Glycoprotein antibodies are highly specific for APS unlike Anticardiolipin antibodies which may be positive in certain infectious diseases\n\nIndications\n· Clinical history suggestive of Arterial or venous thrombosis\n· History of Recurrent fetal loss /Intrauterine growth restriction/ Premature birth",
    "htmlContent": "<p><strong>2006 International Consensus statement on Classification of definite APS<br></strong></p>\n\n<strong>CLINICAL CRITERIA</strong>\n<br><strong>LABORATORY CRITERIA</strong>\n\nArterial/Venous thrombosis\n<br>Cardiolipin antibodies (aCL)\n\nFetal Loss\n<br>Beta 2 Glycoprotein 1 antibodies\n\nPremature birth\n<br>Lupus Anticoagulant (LA) \n\n<p><strong><br>Note:</strong> <br>APS is diagnosed if at least one laboratory criterion and one clinical criterion are met. For the laboratory criteria, they need to be observed on two or more separate occasions, with each test done at least 12 weeks apart. <br><br><strong>Comments</strong> <br>This test checks for IgG antibodies to Beta-2 glycoprotein. Finding these antibodies can indicate a risk of blood clots in arteries and veins, as well as a higher chance of repeated miscarriages. The new International classification criteria for APS include Beta-2 glycoprotein for 2 key reasons namely: <br>1. Beta-2 Glycoprotein antibodies maybe the only antibody present in 10% patients of Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) <br>2. Beta-2 Glycoprotein antibodies are highly specific for APS unlike Anticardiolipin antibodies which may be positive in certain infectious diseases <br><br><strong>Indications</strong> <br>· Clinical history suggestive of Arterial or venous thrombosis <br>· History of Recurrent fetal loss /Intrauterine growth restriction/ Premature birth</p>"
